+\subsection*{Extension to all finite uniformities}
+The finite linear trace argument can be iterated beyond triples.  For a
+finite graph $J$ and an integer $r\ge3$, write $J^{[r]}$ for the hypergraph
+obtained by adding $r-2$ distinct private vertices to every edge of $J$.
+Let $\mathcal B_r$ be the class generated by these expansions of finite
+bipartite graphs and by finite edgeless systems, under finite disjoint
+unions and one-point amalgamations.  As throughout the paper, embeddings
+are injective and non-induced, and proper vertex colourings have no
+monochromatic edge.
+
+\begin{theoremB}
+For every fixed integer $r\ge3$ and every finite simple $r$-uniform
+hypergraph $F$, the following are equivalent:
+\begin{enumerate}
+\item $F$ occurs in every $r$-uniform hypergraph of uncountable chromatic number;
+\item $F\in\mathcal B_r$;
+\item after isolated vertices are removed, $F$ is linear, each hyperedge-node
+of its Levi graph is incident with at least $r-2$ bridges, and every
+Berge cycle has even length.
+\end{enumerate}
+\end{theoremB}
+
+Theorem~\ref{uniform-classification} proves this extension.  Reiher's
+expansion theorem already supplies the positive generators for every
+uniformity \citep[Theorem~1.2]{reiher2024}.  The additional step is the
+finite linear trace lemma with a hypergraph base: projection preserves
+linearity, and one-private-vertex inflation commutes with the forest
+assembly.  Iterating the lift therefore reduces the negative direction to
+the same high-odd-girth graph input used for triples.  The bridge
+requirement increases with $r$; the example in
+Remark~\ref{uniform-four-edge-example} shows why one bridge no longer
+suffices at uniformity four.
+
+The canonical atoms in this generality are single $r$-edges and
+$J^{[r]}$ with $J$ a finite $2$-connected bipartite graph.  Writing
+$s=n-(r-2)m$ and $\beta=(r-1)m-n+c$ transports the finite spectra,
+factorization products and fixed-atom profiles below to every finite
+uniformity (Corollary~\ref{uniform-finite-spectra}).  The new results in
+Section~\ref{uniform-extension} have manuscript proofs; they are not
+additional Lean-verified endpoints.  The detailed triple-system argument
+and its finite structure remain in Sections~1--10.
+
